\chapter{Parser combinators and offset contracts}
\label{ch:54}
\index{Parser combinators and offset contracts}

The source Parsec helpers build around text and byte offsets. A scanner result can carry success, a parsed value, and the next offset. The signed decimal helper includes the smallest i64 and rolls back to the starting position on malformed or overflowing input. A parser composed from several operations should define which operations commit and which failures preserve the original position.

\section{Parse a bounded field}
Test a leading sign with no digits, a maximum in-range field followed by a delimiter, a field overflowing by one, and a start index outside the input. For an optional field, check the next offset even when a default value is returned. An unchanged offset lets an alternative parser reuse the original checkpoint. A successful result with zero width requires care inside repetition, since applying the same parser without progress can loop. The native MuLIB Parsec kernel has its own profile and ownership switches. A source helper's semantics do not select those optional C switches.

\section{Run the decimal loop by hand}
For the input \OnxCodeTerm{x-27,} start at byte one. The sign check consumes the minus and records the position of the first possible digit, two. The accumulator starts at zero. On digit 2 it becomes minus two; on digit 7 it becomes minus twenty-seven. Position four is a comma rather than a digit, and at least one digit was consumed. The answer is therefore success, minus twenty-seven, four. A caller parsing comma-separated fields should test the comma at four, not at five. On the shorter input \OnxCodeTerm{x-,} the first possible digit is already a comma. The result is failure, zero, one: the sign has not committed the alternative.

Why keep a negative accumulator even when the input has no minus? The magnitude of the smallest signed integer does not fit in a positive signed integer. The implementation picks the permitted negative limit from the sign, checks whether multiplication by ten is possible, multiplies, then checks whether subtracting the next digit would pass that limit. Only a successful nonnegative input is negated at the end. Constructing the positive magnitude first and applying the sign afterwards would fail on a valid endpoint of the range.

One final boundary distinguishes two helpers in this module. At the offset exactly equal to the input length, matching an empty literal succeeds: no bytes need comparison. Starting the integer scanner there fails because it needs a digit. A caller that models both operations merely as ``at end means failure'' discards a real distinction.

